  "live_records": {
    "q-111835": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111835",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$6{w}^{2}+19w+10=$",
      "worked_solution": "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n6{w}^{2}+19w+10.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($6\\times 10=60$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($19$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $60.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $60$ are\n\n$$\n(1,60),\\,(2,30),\\,(3,10),\\,(4,15),\\,(5,12),\\,(6,10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(4,15)$ add to $19:$ \n\n$$\n4+15=19\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $4w+15w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{w}^{2}+19w+10 & = \\\\ 6{w}^{2}+4w+15w+10 & = \\\\ (6{w}^{2}+4w)+(15w+10) & = \\\\ 2w(3w+2)+5(3w+2) & = \\\\ (3w+2)(2w+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
