      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$5{b}^{2}-34b-7=(b\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$b\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n5{b}^{2}-34b-7.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($5\\times -7=-35$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-34$).\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of $-35:$\n\n$$\n(1,-35),\\,(-1,35),\\,(5,-7),\\,(-5,7)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,-35)$ add to $-34:$ \n\n$$\n1+(-35)=-34\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $b-35b,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{b}^{2}-34b-7 & = \\\\ 5{b}^{2}+b-35b-7 & = \\\\ (5{b}^{2}+b)-(35b+7) & = \\\\ b(5b+1)-7(5b+1) & = \\\\ (b-7)(5b+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
