    "q-224920": {
      "dom_id": "step-q224920",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$4{x}^{2}-15x+9=(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-15x+9.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($4\\times 9=36$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-15$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $36.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $36$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-36),\\,(-2,-18),\\,(-3,-12),\\,(-4,-9),\\,(-6,-6).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-3,-12)$ add to $-15:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)+(-12) & =-15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-3x-12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-15x+9 & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-3x-12x+9 & = \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-3x)-(12x-9) & = \\\\ x(4x-3)-3(4x-3) & = \\\\ (x-3)(4x-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
